# shellcheck shell=bash
#
# shared/support.lib -- what the support tree carries, for every platform that
# packages it. SOURCED, never run: it is 0644 so `is_handler` (-f AND -x) cannot
# mistake it for a command.
#
# MOVED HERE FROM `cmd/macos` ON 2026-10-12 (ST0081), WITH THE REASONING THAT
# SHAPED IT, BECAUSE A SECOND PLATFORM NOW PACKAGES THE SAME TREE. `cmd/macos`
# archives it as `intent-support.tar.gz`; `cmd/linux` puts it beside the Linux
# binaries in each `intent-<triple>.tar.gz`. One list sourced by both handlers is
# what keeps the two platforms' installs one tree, and a copy in each handler is
# the one-list-two-homes shape the reasoning below records twice.

# THE ONE HOME for what the support tree carries. Relative to $ROOT. Every path
# v3 resolves against its install root is either here or comes from the binaries
# themselves, and `support_paths_coverage` in `cmd/macos` holds that sentence to the
# source rather than to this comment.
#
# THIS LIST WAS `lib/templates` ALONE UNTIL 2026-08-26, AND THE COMMENT THAT KEPT
# IT THAT WAY WAS NOT LAZY -- IT WAS VERIFIED AGAINST THE WRONG POPULATION. It
# read: "everything the shipped consumers reach into is under it -- verified
# rather than assumed: the only path any shipped SCRIPT references outside its
# own tree is `$INTENT_HOME_RESOLVED`". That verification was real and correct
# for shipped scripts. The rules and skills trees are read by the RUST BINARY,
# which was never in the population -- so the check could not have failed, and
# the words `verified rather than assumed` are exactly why nobody looked again.
#
# What it cost: the 3.0.0 keg shipped no rule library and no skills tree, so on
# every project running it `intent critic <lang> --staged` armed 0 of 0 rules,
# printed `ok` and exited 0, and the pre-commit hook branched on that rc
# correctly and passed. Found by devbin/vc as a PACKAGING gap rather than a
# resolver fault, which is the call that pointed here instead of into the rules
# loader; located to this line by vc; resolver enumerated by dc.
#
# A RECORDED VERIFICATION MUST NAME ITS POPULATION, or the next reader inherits
# the confidence without the scope.
#
# `intent/plugins/claude` SHIPS WHOLE, BECAUSE THE BINARY CHOOSES ITS CHILD AT RUN
# TIME. `payload.rs` resolves `intent/plugins/claude/<kind.canon_subdir()>` --
# `skills` or `subagents` -- and no reading of the source can list what a
# run-time join will ask for. Until 2026-09-11 this list named the rules and
# skills trees and the `intent_claude_cwi` file, the coverage guard counted the
# directory as covered by those children, and the 3.0.1 keg shipped with no
# `subagents` tree: `intent claude subagents list` answered `no subagents in this
# install` at exit 0. `support_paths_coverage` now accepts a run-time-resolved
# directory only when the whole directory ships, so the next child cannot be
# left out in silence. The file-level `intent_claude_cwi` entry existed to keep
# v2's plugin scripts out of the keg; they are gone from `bin/`, which now holds
# only the launcher.
#
# HOW `intent_claude_cwi` WENT MISSING FROM THE 3.0.0 KEG, BECAUSE THE ANSWER
# CHANGES WHAT A FIX MEANS. It was not missed when this list was corrected on
# 2026-08-26 (`0112b8c1`). The cwi door
# landed the NEXT DAY, `68ca70bf` 2026-08-27 14:41Z, and the copy list did not
# follow it. `support_paths_coverage` caught it on the first stage attempt after
# that -- **so this entry is the check WORKING, not the check having failed.** A
# list that must be updated by hand when a resolver is added will go stale again;
# what makes that survivable is that the coverage arm reads the consumer, so the
# staleness is refused at stage rather than shipped.
#
# WHAT IT DARKENS WHEN ABSENT: `intent claude start <ws>` -- the MAAC whiteboard
# launcher, `disposition: keep`. It had a sibling, `intent claude ws <verb>`, the
# provisioner, until ST0069 AC-14.12 retired the family; the Whiteboard Protocol
# skill SHIPS IN THIS SAME KEG and named `intent claude ws new` as the
# provisioner, so a keg without this file used to contradict its own
# documentation on disk. The skill now names `intent wb`, which is in the
# binary rather than in this script, so what a missing file darkens is the
# launch and nothing else.
#
# It needs no formula `chmod`: the archive preserves 0755 through `tar`, and the
# `chmod` in the formula exists only for the BINARIES, which GitHub serves as
# bare 644 files. Verified in the archive, not assumed.
# Issue 0340: `intent plugin list` reads each plugin's `plugin.json`, and the
# agents plugin's manifest did not ship, so the keg listed only `claude`.
#
# THE LIST LIVES IN THE BINARY NOW (ST0087 WP-01, the contract's T1), as
# `intentsvcs::install::RUNTIME_SET`, and nothing here keeps a copy: each
# handler reads it from the pair it means with `support_paths_from`, the staged
# or release pair for a release and the packaged pair for a Linux package. A
# pair behind HEAD is dirty by T2 and cannot pass the checks that ask, so an
# older binary's list never decides, and no guard is needed between two homes.

# support_paths_from <intent> -- set SUPPORT_PATHS, the support paths of the
# runtime set as <intent> names them (`intent install --print-runtime-set`),
# space-separated. Refuses when that binary is absent or cannot answer, since a
# support tree read from nowhere is the omission this list exists to prevent.
support_paths_from() {
  local bin="$1" out
  [ -x "$bin" ] || die "there is no intent binary at $bin to read the runtime set from -- build the pair first (bin/devbin build all)"
  # Stdout only, so nothing the binary warns about on stderr becomes a path;
  # its stderr reaches the terminal above the refusal.
  out="$("$bin" install --print-runtime-set)" || die "$bin could not print its runtime set (its stderr is above), so the support tree is unknown"
  [ -n "$out" ] || die "$bin printed an empty runtime set"
  SUPPORT_PATHS="$(printf '%s\n' "$out" | tr '\n' ' ')"
  SUPPORT_PATHS="${SUPPORT_PATHS% }"
}

# support_tree_tar <root> <commit> <prefix> -- the support tree AT <commit>, as
# a tar stream on stdout, every path under <prefix> ("" for none).
#
# ONE HOME FOR "THE TREE A RELEASE SHIPS" (issue 0626), because every platform
# packages the same tree and each must take it from the COMMIT. `git archive`
# reads the commit's tree and nothing else, so no untracked or ignored path, no
# empty directory and no .DS_Store on the packaging machine can reach an asset,
# and the asset is a function of the ref. `cmd/linux` always took it this way;
# `cmd/macos` tarred the working copy, and v3.2.5's intent-support.tar.gz
# carried three directories git does not track. `tar.umask=022` keeps the modes a
# checkout gives them, 0755 and 0644: `git archive`'s own default, 002, writes
# every file group-writable. The Linux package had 0755 and 0644 until now only
# because `tar -x` applied the packaging runner's umask, 022; neither asset
# depends on the packaging machine's umask any more.
support_tree_tar() {
  # SUPPORT_PATHS is a fixed list of three space-free repository paths, and
  # splitting it into one argument each is the point (IN-SH-CODE-001 exempts
  # intentional splitting with a comment; issue 0536).
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  git -c tar.umask=022 -C "$1" archive --format=tar --prefix="$3" "$2" -- $SUPPORT_PATHS
}

# THE INSTALL-ROOT POPULATION, BESIDE THE COPY LIST IT IS HELD AGAINST (ST0081
# WP-06). `cmd/macos`'s stage coverage check asks whether SUPPORT_PATHS carries
# every path below, and `int brew smoke` asks whether a built keg holds them, so
# both read this one copy now that smoke lives in `cmd/brew`. $ROOT is the
# sourcing handler's project root.
#
# What the BINARIES put in the install root, as opposed to the support tarball.
# The formula installs these directly, so the copy list must not be expected to
# carry them and the coverage check in `cmd/macos` must not demand it.
# shellcheck disable=SC2034 # read by the handlers that source this file
INSTALL_BINARY_PATHS="bin"

# resolved_install_paths -- every path v3 resolves against its install root,
# read out of the RESOLVERS. One line per path, sorted, no duplicates.
#
# THE ONE HOME FOR THAT QUESTION, AND IT IS SHARED ON PURPOSE:
# `support_paths_coverage` (does the copy list carry them) and smoke ARM 3 (does
# the built keg contain them). Before 2026-08-29 ARM 3 asked its question of
# `$SUPPORT_PATHS` instead -- the copy list -- so it audited the artefact against
# the very list whose omissions it existed to catch, and would have reported all
# green on a keg missing a tree. That is the SECOND instance in this file of a
# check whose stated claim is wider than its population; the first is the one
# recorded at SUPPORT_PATHS above. Deriving both from here is what stops there
# being a third.
#
# THE WHOLE CHAIN, NOT THE FIRST LINK. `plugins.rs` writes
# `install.join("intent").join("plugins")`, and a pattern that stops at the
# first `.join(...)` reads that as `intent` -- which is covered by any entry
# under `intent/`, so a chained NEW tree would pass while its first segment
# carried it. Found by driving the positive control, not by reading.
#
# POPULATION, NAMED, BECAUSE NAMING IT IS THE WHOLE LESSON OF THE DEFECT: `src/`
# only. A `tests/` join builds a FIXTURE install root -- `skills_sync.rs` mkdirs
# some of these paths in a tempdir -- so a fixture path is not a shipping
# requirement, and reading tests here would refuse a release over a temp dir.
#
# ONE SCAN, TWO KINDS OF LINE, AND A CHAIN MAY SPAN LINES. `install_path_chains`
# reads each resolver file whole, so a chain rustfmt breaks across lines is seen
# -- `payload.rs` writes
#
#   install
#     .join("intent/plugins/claude")
#     .join(kind.canon_subdir()),
#
# and a line-at-a-time grep saw none of it, which is how the 3.0.1 keg shipped
# with no subagents tree while this guard printed nothing. Each line is
#   L<TAB><path>   a literal chain: the binary resolves exactly <path>
#   R<TAB><path>   a literal chain CONTINUED by a non-literal join: the binary
#                  resolves a child of <path> chosen at run time
# An R path is covered only by shipping the WHOLE directory (see below), because
# no reading of the source can say which children the run time will ask for.
install_path_chains() {
  local src="$ROOT/native/rust/crates"
  [ -d "$src" ] || return 0
  find "$src" -path '*/src/*' -name '*.rs' -exec perl -0777 -ne '
    while (/\b(?:install_root|install)((?:\s*\.join\(\s*"[^"]+"\s*\))+)(\s*\.join\(\s*[^\s")])?/g) {
      my ($chain, $more) = ($1, $2);
      my $p = join("/", $chain =~ /"([^"]+)"/g);
      $p =~ s{^/+}{};
      print((defined $more ? "R" : "L"), "\t", $p, "\n");
    }' {} + 2>/dev/null | sort -u
}

resolved_install_paths() {
  install_path_chains | cut -f2 | sort -u
}
